iosurfaceTextureAlignmentBytes and iosurfaceReadOnlyTextureAlignmentBytes answer the alignment of the IOSurface planes the
runtime makes a texture over (16, linear_plan's LINEAR_ALIGNMENT, as Metal on an Apple GPU), not the device's linear alignment,
the largest any format needs to lie in place (256 on Turnip A8xx: RGBA32F; its 4-byte formats lie in place at 64).
deviceLinearTextureAlignmentBytes and linearTextureAlignmentBytes keep the device's, and placement in place is unchanged.

A caller takes the answer as the surfaces it may use, not as a layout to round to: macOS 27's CoreAnimation refuses each
texture over a surface whose rows miss it and draws nothing of the layer (WindowServer: "Failed to allocate MTLTexture from
IOSurface ... Row bytes (5696) not 256 aligned", 6436 times in 6 minutes; Bing's search box, whole local pages missing). Every
row seen there (5696, 3968, 1088, 192, 128, 64) is a multiple of 64: the BGRA8 ones lie in place all the same.

